{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790714736,"nonce":"8yUdBYaSbRHn+jA06JShhl+CO5A=","message":"Construction overhead was reduced further in Zyntax 685d64cc and Caribou 0d86e32.\n\nA successful host constructor now uses its required non-null result to skip thread-local error lookup. Caribou core objects store their compact Handle directly in the Zyntax foreign box's opaque word instead of allocating a separate Held::Object, and constructor results bypass generic result classification.\n\nFor 60 million constructions per profile, sampled-thread CPU fell from 12.486 s to 6.790 s for Python→Haxe (-45.6%) and from 15.120 s to 11.194 s for Python→Wren (-26.0%). A paired post-change run under contention measured 189.3 ns and 195.6 ns respectively.\n\nThe remaining representation cost is one DynamicBox and one Caribou root handle per escaping host object. Removing both needs a typed, unboxed HostObject value in Zyntax IR with an explicit release operation and a root visible to Caribou's collector. Python's own approximately 5 ns construction cell cannot serve as the allocation baseline because the optimizer scalar-replaces that local object.\n\nThe Zyntax foreign integration test and Caribou release python_foreign/python_haxe tests pass.","files":null}]}